{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_22","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,220p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed2085404187/2/prompt.md\"","aggregated_output":"你是一个 ARC-AGI 谜题设计专家。下面的 20 道参考题是难度和机制质量的标杆。仔细阅读每道题的**解题逻辑**（解题者从样例归纳规则的认知过程），而非表面场景。你的新题应在机制深度和隐蔽性上与参考题持平或者实现超越。\n\n每个参考题描述包含：\n- `description`：一句话概述题目的变换目标。\n- `Input structure`：输入网格中有哪些对象、背景、标记、边界或组件。\n- `Rule`：从输入到输出的确定性变换规则。\n- `Key insight`：解题所需的核心洞见。\n\n参考题目如下：\n<reference_puzzle index=\"1\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"136b0064\" lines=\"25-39\">\n<description>\nDraw a polyline by decoding 3x3 glyph instructions read across a divided grid.\n\nInput structure:\n- Left of a full-height vertical divider: 3x3 colored glyphs on bg.\n- Right of divider: a single gray (5) start pixel.\n\nRule:\n- Remove the divider; shift the gray start into the left canvas.\n- Read glyphs in column-major order (left→right, top→bottom).\n- Each glyph's shape (pixel area) encodes a segment: area 4 → down 2, area 5 → right 3, area 6 → left 4, area 7 → left 2.\n- Append segments sequentially, stepping down one cell between each.\n\nKey insight: Glyph pixel count (area) uniquely determines both segment length and direction — the shape itself is the instruction.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"2\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"cb2d8a2c\" lines=\"13-26\">\n<description>\nExtend a green path from one grid edge to the opposite, routing around red bars at a distance dictated by their blue pixel count.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = azure (8). Red/blue bars (color 2/1) act as obstacles.\n- A single green (3) pixel sits on a grid border — the path start.\n\nRule:\n- Each bar's keep-out radius equals its number of blue pixels; the path must stay farther than that from every bar cell.\n- The green pixel extends inward toward the opposite edge, turning at right angles to navigate around forbidden zones.\n- After the path is drawn, all blue pixels become red.\n\nKey insight: Blue pixels on a bar encode its danger radius — more blue means a wider no-go zone the path must respect.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"3\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"88bcf3b4\" lines=\"229-241\">\n<description>\nSwing an irregular line segment around a nearby obstacle sprite, preserving its pixel count.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg, bar color (straight lines perpendicular to the grid edge, one end on the border), segment color (irregular stroke attached to bar's inner endpoint), obstacle color (sprite near the bar terminal).\n\nRule:\n- Each bar is perpendicular to the edge it touches; its inner (terminal) endpoint has an attached irregular segment.\n- The segment detaches and re-routes: it travels from the terminal toward the nearest obstacle, hugs the obstacle's boundary, then exits at a 45° diagonal.\n- Total painted pixels equal the original segment length.\n\nKey insight: The segment behaves like a rope swung around the obstacle — attach at the nearest point, trace the skin, exit diagonally, conserving length.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"4\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"5961cc34\" lines=\"8-21\">\n<description>\nBuild a red ray network by activating blue blobs via directional green ports.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color. Large blue blobs with adjacent green pixels marking port directions.\n- Start marker: yellow pixel atop a short red segment at grid bottom.\n\nRule:\n- Extend a ray upward from start; on hitting a blue blob, recolor it red.\n- Activated blobs emit thick rays through green ports until boundary or another blob.\n- Cascade until no new blobs are reached.\n\nKey insight: Green pixels encode ray direction and width; the network activates as a chain reaction from one start point.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"5\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"53fb4810\" lines=\"105-119\">\n<description>\nExtend marker tiles outward from '+' shapes by repeating them to the grid edge.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color.\n- A dominant non-bg color forms several '+'-shaped objects.\n- Small colored patches (1–few cells) sit adjacent to each arm of a '+'.\n\nRule:\n- For each marker tile touching a '+', determine which side of the '+' it sits on.\n- Project the tile away from the '+' in that direction, repeating the tile pattern periodically until the grid boundary.\n- '+' pixels are never overwritten.\n\nKey insight: Each marker's attachment side determines its extension direction; the pattern repeats periodically, not as a single copy.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"6\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"7c66cb00\" lines=\"9-24\">\n<description>\nDock two-tone sprites into color-matched horizontal bars with a color swap.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color.\n- Top area: small two-tone sprites (outer color + inner color).\n- Below: horizontal bars spanning most of the row, each with a fill color and border color on edges.\n\nRule:\n- Match each sprite to the bar whose fill color equals the sprite's outer color.\n- Place the sprite at its original x-position, aligned to the bar's bottom.\n- Recolor: sprite outer → bar border, sprite inner → bar fill.\n- Erase original sprites from the top area.\n\nKey insight: Color matching is inverted — the sprite's dominant color identifies its target bar, then gets swapped to the bar's accent color.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"7\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"31f7f899\" lines=\"7-21\">\n<description>\nSort vertical bars by height and redistribute them across fixed colored slots.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg background; a horizontal base line of a single color spans the full grid width.\n- Several monochrome vertical bars of distinct colors rise upward from the base line.\n\nRule:\n- Rank bar shapes by height (shortest to tallest).\n- Rank original x-positions (slots) left to right.\n- Place the i-th shortest shape into the i-th leftmost slot, recoloring it to that slot's original color.\n- The base line is preserved unchanged.\n\nKey insight: Each slot keeps its x-position and color identity; only the bar silhouettes are redistributed among slots by height order.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"8\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"4c3d4a41\" lines=\"15-28\">\n<description>\nFit a grey bar into a grey frame by aligning projections with colored columns, then stack colors above.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg, a grey structural color forming two components: a large rectangular frame (slot) and a smaller bar with upward projections.\n- Colored pixels inside the slot mark target columns.\n\nRule:\n- Move the bar upward into the slot so its bottom edge sits one cell above the slot's interior bottom.\n- Shift it horizontally so the bar's upward projections align column-wise with the colored pixels in the slot.\n- Stack the colored pixels immediately above the bar projections in each column.\n\nKey insight: The bar's projections act as keys that must align with the colored-column \"locks\" in the slot.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"9\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"3dc255db\" lines=\"37-53\">\n<description>\nEject content pixels from cup-shaped frames outward along each cup's tail direction.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = background. Hollow cup/Y/funnel frames, each a unique border color.\n- Each cup has a convergent tail spike on one side and an open mouth on the opposite side.\n- Content pixels (a single non-border color per cup) sit inside the cup's mouth interior.\n\nRule:\n- For each cup, identify its tail spike and tail direction.\n- Erase all content pixels from the mouth interior.\n- Draw a straight line of content-colored pixels from the spike outward in the tail direction,\n  one pixel per erased content pixel; cells beyond the grid edge are dropped.\n\nKey insight: The tail spike is the unique convergent point whose outward direction is opposite\nthe mouth opening where the content lives.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"10\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"800d221b\" lines=\"7-22\">\n<description>\nRecolor a path-network by adjacent seed regions.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color.\n- Path color P forms a thin orthogonal network with a solid square hub (NxN).\n- Seed colors appear in colored regions (non-bg, non-P) adjacent to path branches.\n\nRule:\n- Infer P as the non-bg color whose largest component is most network-like.\n- Find the largest solid k×k all-P square (the hub); exclude it from recoloring.\n- Recolor each path branch to the seed-region color it touches most.\n- Recolor the hub center pixel to the seed whose branches touch the hub most.\n\nKey insight: The hub stays uncolored; branches inherit color from adjacent seed regions by adjacency count.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"11\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"221dfab4\" lines=\"6-19\">\n<description>\nPropagate a repeating striped pattern from a yellow border segment toward the opposite edge, recoloring foreground objects on certain stripes.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color. Foreground blobs of another color scattered in the interior.\n- A short yellow (4) segment sits on one border edge.\n\nRule:\n- From the yellow segment, march toward the opposite edge in steps of 2 cells, cycling colors Y, Y, G (4, 4, 3). The 1-cell gap between stripes is forced to bg.\n- Yellow stripes paint only within the segment's span.\n- Green stripes additionally recolor all non-bg pixels across the entire row/column to green.\n\nKey insight: Green stripes act as a \"flood\" that recolors existing objects on the full row/column, while yellow stripes are confined to the segment span.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"12\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"1818057f\" lines=\"6-19\">\n<description>\nDetect thin '+' shapes in a single foreground color and recolor them to teal.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color.\n- One foreground color (e.g. yellow) forms various shapes on the bg, some of which are small '+' crosses.\n\nRule:\n- A '+' center is any foreground pixel whose 4 orthogonal neighbors are also foreground.\n- Recolor each such center and its 4 neighbors to teal (8).\n- All other pixels remain unchanged.\n\nKey insight: Only compact 5-cell crosses qualify — a pixel embedded in a larger filled region also satisfies the neighbor check, so the rule highlights every local '+' motif, not just isolated ones.\n</description>\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
